#1 Math and references

StructMark treats mathematics and document references as distinct semantic structures. Formulas, heading identifiers, footnotes and external links should not be conflated. This page describes the 1.1 subset; complete LaTeX and CSL style compatibility are not promised.

#2 Inline math and scripts

`$...$` preserves TeX source. `^[...]` and `_[...]` are StructMark's own superscript and subscript syntax. KaTeX rendering is opt-in and only supports the TeX constructs handled by that engine.

```sm
Use x^[2] for superscript and H_[2]O for subscript.

Formula: $E=mc^2$
```

#2 Display math

Place `$$` on separate lines around a display formula. The `&equation` raw directive supports an optional identifier; inline formatting is not interpreted inside the raw body.

```sm
$$
E = mc^2
$$

&equation[#energy]
E = mc^2
&end
```

#2 Headings and IDs

Add `(#id)` at the end of a heading. Use `&ref[id]` to reference a heading. Optional numbering can label headings, figures, tables, and equations.

```sm
#2 Processing pipeline (#pipeline)

Read &ref[pipeline] for more details.
```

#2 Cross-document references

Use `path.sm#id` and the workspace validation API; the parser cannot verify missing files in isolation. Explicit `./` and `../` paths make relative intent unambiguous.

```sm
See &ref[./reference.sm#definition].

Also see &ref[../overview.sm#intro].
```

#2 Footnotes and citations

Footnotes use `[^id]`; references to literature use `[&key]`. BibTeX and CSL JSON ingestion are available, but the built-in author-year, APA, and IEEE profiles are simplified deterministic formatters rather than complete citation style engines.

```sm
A footnote[^note] appears here.

See also [&smith2024].

[^note]: The footnote definition.
```
